{"message":{"container":null,"content":[{"caller":{"type":"direct"},"id":"toolu_01KAeqqV8HAx2gaM3BhzYf1v","input":{"command":"driver-exec signal comment <<'SPINDRIFT_SIGNAL_EOF'\n[REDACTED:inline-code]\nSPINDRIFT_SIGNAL_EOF"},"name":"Bash","type":"tool_use"}],"context_management":null,"diagnostics":null,"id":"msg_011Cfi5Yhgqr8cQ1amoDCXhR","input_transformations":[],"model":"claude-opus-5-5","role":"assistant","stop_details":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"type":"message","usage":{"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":666,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"cache_creation_input_tokens":666,"cache_read_input_tokens":40156,"inference_geo":"not_available","input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":7,"service_tier":"standard"}},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"request_id":"req_011Cfi5YhShSobBenjXJdj6h","session_id":"0151da83-89ef-da76-8cd0-4f8aa67cb841","timestamp":"2026-10-04T23:39:42.692Z","type":"assistant","uuid":"174febb3-d637-4329-b373-6c46b6e04e9c","wire_ingest_context":{"toolu_01KAeqqV8HAx2gaM3BhzYf1v":{"cwd":"/work"}},"wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01KAeqqV8HAx2gaM3BhzYf1v":{"command":"cd /work && driver-exec signal comment <<'SPINDRIFT_SIGNAL_EOF'\n<!-- spindrift-research -->\n## Research verdict: `recommend`\n\nThe bug is real and reproduces on `main` (0aa1ca0f). Both examples in the issue misclassify a deterministic failure as transient, so the launcher retries it.\n\n## Context for a worker\n\n**Where the bug is**\n\n- `cmd/launcher/internal/driver/opencode/classify.go:20-26`: `transientExtras` lists the bare markers `{Substr: \"429\", Reason: RateLimit}` and `{Substr: \"529\", Reason: Overloaded}`.\n- `cmd/launcher/internal/driver/opencode/classify.go:50`: the extract hook returns `ScanDecision{Text: s}`, where `s` is the whole trimmed JSON line. That means `timestamp`, `sessionID`, and any other metadata are matched too, not just the error.\n- `cmd/launcher/internal/driver/driverkit/classify.go:47` sends that text to `MatchTransient`. `driverkit/patterns.go:38-44` matches with plain `strings.Contains` and has no anchoring.\n- `cmd/launcher/internal/dispatch/retry.go:44`: a `Transient` class keeps the retry loop going, so a deterministic failure uses up the retry budget before it reaches `agent-failed`.\n- For comparison, the claude driver (`cmd/launcher/internal/driver/claude/classify.go:46-62`) already uses phrase markers (`\"429 Too Many Requests\"`, `\"529 Overloaded\"`). Its comment says why: \"so they do not match ordinary log content that happens to contain digit sequences\". opencode is the only driver with bare numeric markers.\n\n**Repro (throwaway test in `package opencode_test`, using the existing `opencode.WriteLog` helper)**\n\n```\n{\"type\":\"error\",\"error\":\"prompt is too long: 205290 tokens > 200000 maximum\"}\n  -> transient/overloaded\n{\"type\":\"error\",\"timestamp\":1759542912345,\"error\":{\"name\":\"UnknownError\",\"data\":{\"message\":\"boom\"}}}\n  -> transient/rateLimit\n```\n\nBoth lines should classify as `terminal/taskFailed`.\n\n**Things to watch**\n\n- **Error shape.** The existing tests write `error` as a plain string. Real `opencode run --format json` error events most likely carry an object (a NamedError-style `{\"name\":…,\"data\":{\"message\":…,\"statusCode\":…}}`) next to top-level `timestamp` and `sessionID`. Please confirm the shape against opencode's `run` command source or a captured log before choosing what to decode. Whatever you extract needs to handle both the string form and the object form.\n- **Pin the precedence.** `TestClassify_ErrorEvent_LooseDigit429BeatsOverloaded` (`classify_test.go:55-72`) checks that `\"overloaded_error: upstream returned status 429\"` classifies as RateLimit (issue #2149). An anchored marker such as `status 429` or a digit-boundary match keeps this passing. If the fix changes that behaviour, update the test on purpose rather than by accident.\n- **Choose an anchoring approach.** `driverkit.Pattern` only supports substrings. You have two options:\n  - (a) Use phrase markers the way claude does, for example `429 Too Many Requests`, `status 429`, `\"statusCode\":429`, and the 529 equivalents.\n  - (b) Add digit-boundary matching to the opencode extract or match step, for example `(^|[^0-9])429([^0-9]|$)`.\n\n  Option (a) keeps driverkit untouched. Avoid widening driverkit's shared API unless a second driver needs it.\n\n**Acceptance criteria**\n\n1. Only the error payload is matched, not the whole line. Event metadata such as `timestamp` and `sessionID` can no longer produce a transient match.\n2. The numeric 429/529 markers are anchored, so a digit run like `205290` or `1759542912345` does not match.\n3. New tests cover both repro lines above and expect `Terminal/TaskFailed`. They cover the string and object `error` shapes, and a timestamp that contains `429`.\n4. The existing opencode classify tests stay green, including the RateLimit-over-overloaded precedence test and the last-error-event-wins test.\n5. The `transientExtras` doc comment (`classify.go:16-19`) is updated, since it currently justifies the bare markers.\n\n**Related issues**\n\n- #2149: the precedence pin.\n- #2251 / #2269: the driverkit extraction that produced this shape.\n- I found no duplicate open or closed issue.\nSPINDRIFT_SIGNAL_EOF"}}}
